  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 150 PM CDT Mon Oct 19 2026\n\n18Z water vapor imagery showed a closed upper low off the coast of \nthe Pacific Northwest with a broad upper ridge along the gulf coast. \nThis placed southwesterly upper flow across the central plains with a\nweak wave lifting through north central KS. Some mid level moisture \nand lift have combined for some elevated showers with mainly trace \namounts of moisture reaching the ground. At the surface low pressure \nin the lee of the Rockies and high pressure along the MS river valley\nhave favored southerly low level flow. However moisture return from \nthe gulf coast has be slow. \n\nFor tonight through Wednesday, a second more organized shortwave \nthen today's wave is progged to round the top of the upper ridge \nover the forecast area Tuesday night. This with some continued \nisentropic lift and better mid level moisture advection could lead \nto some isolated elevated showers into Wednesday morning. Mid level \nlapse rate are forecasts to only be around 6 C/km so have not \nincluded a mention of thunder, but wouldn't be surprised if there \nare a few rumbles of thunder. With the slower moisture return than \nthe models expected and skies clearing out tonight as the wave lifts \nnorth of the area, have adjusted MinT grids a little lower closer to \nMOS guidance thinking there should be reasonable radiational \ncooling. Highs Tuesday and Wednesday are forecast to gradually trend \nwarmer a few degrees each day. Overall there is pretty good \nagreement in the deterministic solutions as well as the ensembles. \nSo have not strayed to far from the NBM. \n\nUpper ridging is progged to set up for the end of the week with no \nobvious forcing for vertical motion. So the forecast continues to \ncall for dry weather with temps continuing a slight warming trend. \nFor Saturday through Monday, the spread in potential solutions \nincreases quite a bit as models struggle to handle the upper trough \ncoming out of the west. The main idea is for a weak frontal boundary \nto move into the plains Saturday or Sunday. So some small chances \nfor precip and a cooling trend seem to be a good step and this is \nshown in the NBM solution. Though the details on when the front \nimpacts the area and it's affect on temps will probably change from \nday to day until it becomes more obvious how the pattern will \nevolve.",
